{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792949358,"nonce":"gjtA1FREymxrLgheZCNMeE6gE2E=","title":"wasm: Float values passed as I64 slot bits lose their high 32 bits","message":"The typed array calls carry an element as raw 64-bit slot bits typed IR `I64`: `haxe_array_set_typed` (the slow path of every `arr[i] = v` with a known element descriptor, lvalue.rs `build_array_store`), `push_typed`, and the `get_typed`/`take_typed`/`get_erased`/`unbox_generic_return` results. The wasm backend lowers IR `I64` to wasm i32 (`ir_type_to_wasm`: everything but F32/F64/NullInt/vectors is i32), so a Float's bit pattern is wrapped to its low word at the call.\n\nReproducer (`rayzor run --wasm`): `var a = new Array\u003cFloat\u003e(); a.push(1.5); a[1] = 2.5; trace(a[1]);` prints 0, native 2.5. `[for (i in 0...3) i * 1.5]` gives `[0,0,0]`. spectralnorm prints `x1e9 = 0`.\n\n`Null\u003cInt\u003e` had the same problem and got `IrType::NullInt` (wasm i64). Options: a distinct IR type for slot bits that every backend treats as I64 and wasm as i64, with runtime-wasm's `*_typed` exports taking i64; or lowering IR `I64` to wasm i64 generally and porting the runtime-wasm exports that take it. Not a per-function pin in wasm_backend.","files":null}]}